return sk ? sk : sk1;
lock_sock(parent);
if (test_bit(BT_SK_DEFER_SETUP, &bt_sk(parent)->flags))
Reason:
The socket returned by `rfcomm_get_sock_by_channel()` is not pinned with `sock_hold()`, so after dropping the list lock, a concurrent close can free the socket. Subsequently, `lock_sock(parent)` in `rfcomm_connect_ind()` operates on freed memory. Additionally, reading `parent` flags after `release_sock()` without a reference can access freed memory.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
sock_hold(sk);
sock_hold(sk1);
sock_put(sk1);
if (parent->sk_state != BT_LISTEN)
    goto done;
defer_setup = test_bit(BT_SK_DEFER_SETUP, &bt_sk(parent)->flags);
sock_put(parent);
Reason:
`sock_hold()` is added to increment the reference count of the listener socket before leaving the list lock, preventing premature free. After acquiring `lock_sock(parent)`, the state is rechecked to ensure the socket is still listening, avoiding use of an invalid socket. The `BT_SK_DEFER_SETUP` flag is cached while the socket is locked, so it remains valid after `release_sock()`. Finally, `sock_put()` releases the extra reference taken earlier, properly cleaning up.

